# configure rewrites this template into zlib.pc (the zlib.pc sed block near
# the end of configure), win32/Makefile.gcc performs the equivalent
# substitution at install time, and Makefile.in installs the result into
# $(libdir)/pkgconfig.  The CMake build uses the parallel template
# zlib.pc.cmakein rather than this file.
#
# This descriptor is implementation-agnostic for DYNAMIC linking: the flags
# below describe the C implementation and the memory-safe Rust implementation
# (crates/libz-rs-sys, built as a cdylib and a staticlib) equally well there,
# so no Rust-specific variant of this file is needed for that case.  What the
# Rust packaging is REQUIRED to reproduce, and what Makefile.in's `rust' target
# implements:
#
#   * the artifacts are named libz.so and libz.a, so -lz resolves to either;
#   * the shared object carries SONAME libz.so.1, and the names around it are
#     laid out exactly as the C build lays them out: the real file is
#     libz.so.1.3.2.1-motley, and libz.so.1 and libz.so are two symlinks
#     pointing DIRECTLY at it -- not a chain in which one alias resolves through
#     the other, which is what `ln -s $(SHAREDLIBV)' does twice for the C
#     library further down and what the `rust' target does twice for this one.
#     A program already linked against libz then keeps loading it under the name
#     recorded in its DT_NEEDED entry, whichever of the three that is.  Cargo
#     produces none of this: it writes a bare libz.so, and the `rust' target
#     relinks the staticlib into the versioned name -- through $(LDSHARED), so
#     the soname and the zlib.map version script are the C build's own -- and
#     symlinks the other two onto it, in a packaging directory of its own rather
#     than in cargo's output directory;
#   * the exported symbol surface must match the C library's 111 dynamic
#     globals, being 95 functions plus the 16 symbol-version nodes declared in
#     zlib.map.  Three things diff that with nm, each running on its own: the
#     suite `crates/libz-rs-sys/tests/symbol_parity.rs' inside `cargo test', the
#     `rust' target while it stages the library, and the symbol-parity job of
#     .github/workflows/rust.yml, which also demands the direct diff against a
#     built C library.  `make rust-symbols' is how to demand that diff by hand;
#   * the installed zlib.h and generated zconf.h are byte-identical, because
#     the Rust build modifies neither header.
#
# Libs: advertising -lz and Cflags: advertising the include directory are part
# of that contract -- do not reword them, and do not add Requires: entries,
# -lm, -lpthread or any Rust-specific flag to them.
#
# ONE PACKAGING DIFFERENCE, AND IT IS REAL: STATIC LINKING.
#
# The C libz.a needs no companion libraries, which is why there is no
# Libs.private: line below and why adding one would be wrong for the C build.
# A Rust staticlib is different -- it carries the Rust standard library's own
# object code, whose undefined references reach into libc and its neighbours.
# On x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu the set is
#
#     -lgcc_s -lutil -lrt -lpthread -lm -ldl -lc
#
# but that is an illustration, not a constant: the set is TARGET-SPECIFIC and
# rustc is the only thing that knows it.
#
# This template cannot carry it, and the reason is specific to THIS build path
# rather than general.  configure expands this file, and configure runs before
# anyone decides whether to run `make rust' at all -- it does not even require
# cargo to be present -- so at substitution time there is nothing to substitute.
# Adding a placeholder would also need a matching rule in configure's zlib.pc sed
# block and a matching -e in win32/Makefile.gcc, for a value that is empty on
# every C build.
#
# So THE AUTOTOOLS PATH ADVERTISES IT FROM A DESCRIPTOR OF ITS OWN, generated
# beside the staged library rather than substituted into this file.  `make rust'
# asks rustc once and records the answer in
# target/dropin/native-static-libs.stage; `make rust-pc' turns that one answer
# into target/dropin/zlib.pc -- this descriptor with a single Libs.private: line
# added and every other field byte for byte -- then compiles, links and runs a C
# program from nothing but the flags pkg-config answers for it, and prints the
# command that installs it over the installed zlib.pc.  `make rust-test' runs
# that gate as part of its own.  So a static consumer keeps writing the link
# command it always wrote, and it is `pkg-config --static' that changes its
# answer rather than the consumer: the historical `Libs: ... -lz' line is
# untouched on every path.
#
# Installing that descriptor is a deliberate step rather than something `make
# install' does, because the autotools path STAGES the Rust library and never
# installs it -- `make install' installs the C libraries, and a descriptor
# advertising a Rust archive's requirements beside a C libz.a would describe
# something that is not there.
#
# The CMake path, which knows at configure time whether the Rust library is
# being built, needs no separate file: zlib.pc.cmakein carries a substitutable
# Libs.private: that is populated there and absent in C mode, CMakeLists.txt
# installs exactly one descriptor at <libdir>/pkgconfig/zlib.pc, and it
# publishes the same set on the exported ZLIB::ZLIBSTATIC target as well.
#
# Dynamic linking is unaffected on every path: the shared object records its own
# dependencies in DT_NEEDED, so `-lz` alone remains correct there.
#
# Two constraints the substitution passes impose on future edits: every
# name=value line must begin in column 0, because configure's rules are
# anchored (/^name *=/s#=.*#=$name#) and would silently skip an indented
# line; and adding a substitutable variable also requires a matching rule in
# configure's zlib.pc sed block -- a different block from the one that
# rewrites Makefile.in -- plus a matching -e in win32/Makefile.gcc.  Prefer
# not to add one.  Never spell a substitution placeholder literally in these
# comments either: both passes substitute globally and would rewrite it here.

prefix=@prefix@
exec_prefix=@exec_prefix@
libdir=@libdir@
sharedlibdir=@sharedlibdir@
includedir=@includedir@

Name: zlib
Description: zlib compression library
Version: @VERSION@
License: Zlib

Requires:
Libs: -L${libdir} -L${sharedlibdir} -lz
Cflags: -I${includedir}
